===Etymology===
From {{inh|en|enm|malencolie}}, from {{der|en|fro|melancolie}}, from {{uder|en|grc|μελαγχολία|t=[[atrabiliousness]]}} (from {{m|grc|μέλας}}, {{m|grc|μελαν-|t=black, dark, murky}} + {{m|grc|χολή|t=bile}}), referring to the humour which ancient {{w|Hippocrates|Hippocratic}} and later {{w|Galen|Galenic}} medicine associated with sadness and despondency. Compare the {{cog|la|ātra bīlis||black bile}}. The adjectival use is a Middle English innovation, perhaps influenced by the suffixes {{m|en|-y}}, {{m|en|-ly}}. {{doublet|en|melancholia}}.

===Pronunciation===
