* from an {{glossary|attributive}} use of {{m|enm|melow}}, {{m|enm|melowe}}, {{m|enm|melewe}}, {{m|enm|mele|t=meal from ground grain or legumes; flour; kernel of barley or lentils}}{{nb...|mel, melu, meluwe|otherforms=1}},<ref>{{R:MED Online|entry=mēle|pos=''n.''(1)|noformat=1|id=MED27242}}</ref><ref>{{R:Lexico|pos=adj}}</ref> from {{inh|en|ang|melo}}, {{m|ang|melu|t=meal (edible part of a grain or pulse); flour}}, from {{inh|en|gem-pro|*melwą|t=ground corn; meal; flour}}, from {{der|en|ine-pro|*melh₂-|id=grind|t=to crush; to grind}}; or
* a variant of {{inh|en|enm|merow}}, {{m|enm|merowe}}, {{m|enm|meruw|t=soft, tender; of a person: frail; of love: unstable, variable}}{{nb...|mearewe, mearuw, mereuh, meru, meruwe, meruȝ, meruʒ, merwe, (Early Middle English) mearwe|otherforms=1}},<ref>{{R:MED Online|entry=meruw(e|pos=adj|id=MED27472}}</ref> from {{inh|en|ang|meru}}, {{m|ang|mearu|t=soft, tender; delicate, frail; callow}}{{nb...|mæru, mearu, mearwe, merwe, myru|otherforms=1}}, from {{inh|en|gem-pro|*marwaz|t=soft, mellow; brittle, delicate}}, from {{inh|en|ine-pro|*mer(w)-|t=to rub; to pack}}.<ref>Compare {{R:OED Online|entry=† merrow|pos=adj|id=116899|date=December 2020}}</ref>

The {{glossary|noun}} and {{glossary|verb}} are both derived from the adjective.<ref>{{R:OED Online|pos=''n.''{{sup|3}}|noformat=1|id=254195|date=June 2020}}</ref><ref>{{R:OED Online|pos=v|id=116201|date=September 2021}}</ref> The etymology of noun sense 3 (“close friend; lover”) is unknown, but may also be derived from the adjective.<ref>{{R:OED Online|pos=''n.''{{sup|2}}|noformat=1|id=251711|date=September 2019}}</ref>

