##* {{quote-book|en|author=[[w:James Gates Percival|James G[ates] Percival]]|chapter=Canto XXVII|title=Prometheus, Part II: With Other Poems|location=New Haven, Conn.|publisher={{...|Printed by}} A. H. Maltby and Co.|year=1822|page=18|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=uKNgAAAAcAAJ&pg=PA18|oclc=13191824|passage=It was from gazing on the fairy hues, / That hung around the born and dying day; / The tender flush, whose '''mellow''' stain imbues / Heaven with all freaks of light, and where it lay / Deep-bosom'd in a still and waveless bay, / The sea reflected all that glow'd above, {{...}}}}
