##* {{RQ:T. Herbert Travaile|chapter=Tyroan|page=115|passage=This City is built of white Sun-burnt brickes, is watered with a ſmall ſtreame, which runs in two parts through the Towne, and '''meloes''' moſt of the Gardens and Groues within her, whereby ſhee yeelds a thankfull tribute of ſundry fruits.}}
##* {{RQ:Evelyn Sylva|chapter=II|chaptername=Of the Seminary|page=6|passage=Having therefore made choice of ſome fit place of ''Ground'', {{...}} let it be ''Broken up'' the ''Winter'' before you ſow, to '''mellow''' it, eſpecially if it be a ''Clay'', and then the ''furrow'' would be made deeper; {{...}}}}
## {{lb|en|figuratively}}
