{{word|en|ine|h₁éǵʰs}}{{root|en|ine-pro|*mend-}}<!--doubtful/unc root-->
From {{inh|en|enm|menden|t=to cure; to do good to, benefit; to do or make better, improve; to get better, recover; to keep in a good state; to put right, amend; to reform, repent}},<ref>{{R:MED Online|entry=mē̆nden|pos=v|id=MED27302}}</ref> the {{glossary|aphetic}} form of {{m|enm|amenden|t=to alter, change (especially for the better); to atone; to chastise, punish; to correct, remedy, amend; to cure; to excel, surpass; to forgive; to get or make better, improve; to make ready; to mend, repair, restore; to get well, recover; to relieve}},<ref>{{R:MED Online|entry=amē̆nden|pos=v|id=MED1394}}</ref> or from its {{glossary|etymon}} {{der|en|xno|amender}} and {{der|en|fro|amender|t=to cure; to fix, repair; to set right, correct}} (modern {{cog|fr|amender}}),<ref>{{R:OED Online|pos=v|id=3048625136|date=March 2024|nodot=1}}; {{R:Lexico|pos=v}}</ref> from {{der|en|la|ēmendāre}}, the {{glossary|present}} {{glossary|active}} {{glossary|infinitive}} of {{m|la|ēmendō|t=to atone; to chastise, punish; to correct, remedy, amend; to cure}}, from {{m|la|ē-}} (variant of {{m|la|ex-|pos={{glossary|prefix}} meaning ‘away; out’}}) + {{m|la|mendum|t=defect; error, fault}} (from {{der|en|ine-pro|*mend-|t=defect; fault}}) + {{m|la|-ō|pos={{glossary|suffix}} forming first-{{glossary|conjugation}} {{glossary|verb}}s}}.

